WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:03.130
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:07.230
Finding Dory director Andrew Stanton has said
there are about 30 to 40 minutes of scenes

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.630
that were deleted from the movie.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.849
For example, the Tank Gang was originally
going to appear in the main part of the film

00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:16.309
and there are also interesting scenes in the
trailers that ended up being cut.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.970
So, in this video, I'm going to take a look
at some of the fascinating deleted scenes

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:23.180
and rejected concepts that didn't make it
into the final film.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.380
Just before I start, I've got a second copy
of this really beautiful hardback book "The

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.260
Art of Finding Dory" to give away thanks to
my friends at Abrams &amp; Chronicle.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.160
It's packed with gorgeous concept art and
information about how the movie was made.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.280
For a chance to win, it's very simple, just
subscribe and leave a comment about the deleted

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.480
scenes on this video.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.620
And of course, there are spoilers ahead, so
if you want to avoid those, right here's where

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.750
you'll find my non-spoiler review of the movie!

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.660
When we see the adult Dory for the first time
in the movie, she's talking to herself while

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.280
asleep in her coral home.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.540
Still asleep, she seems about to sleep swim
out of her home, but she bumps her head and

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.540
wakes up.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.090
Well in the very first trailer for Finding
Dory, we see an alternate version of this

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:08.990
scene where Dory also says "don't cry mommy"
in her sleep and then Nemo wakes up to find

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.189
Dory has gone sleep swimming.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:15.000
This scene which was cut from the final movie
was actually one of the first sequences of

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.189
the film that went into production and was
part of a much longer montage of Dory sleep

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:21.299
swimming which had been storyboarded.

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:25.380
In the storyboarded montage, after Marlin
pulls Dory off some coral that she's stuck

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.320
on, she swims off still asleep.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.549
As Dory swims, she picks up a piece of kelp
which catches and tangles up all the other

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:33.820
fish around her as she swims through the neighbourhood.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:39.079
In one version, the "fish police" are despatched
to try and stop her, with Dory eventually

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:43.079
ending up at the drop-off with all the neighbourhood
fish tangled up behind her.

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:47.739
Another version shows Marlin tying himself
and Nemo to Dory and getting dragged out of

00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:51.739
their home when Dory starts sleep-swimming,
and then the kelp line connecting them gets

00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:56.799
cut accidentally by a swordfish who happens
to swim by.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:01.340
At first, the beginning to Finding Dory was
going to include all of Dory's backstory,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.450
much like the way Marlin's backstory was told
at the start of Finding Nemo.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.899
Although this technique worked well in Finding
Nemo to make us empathise with Marlin, after

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:13.540
test screenings of Finding Dory at Disney,
the filmmakers found that they were telling

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.720
too much of Dory's past at the start of the
film.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.270
Director Andrew Stanton realised he needed
to let the audience experience Dory's backstory

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.569
as flashbacks little by little throughout
the film.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:29.010
And the flashbacks were actually also more
like memory recalls for Dory, which helped

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:33.760
us as an audience experience her story with
her.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.120
You might remember Dory's line from Finding
Nemo "I suffer from short-term memory loss.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.120
It runs in my family.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.079
At least, I think it does", which Dory repeats
again in this sequel.

00:02:43.079 --> 00:02:47.420
Well, the filmmakers initially planned for
both of Dory's parents to also suffer from

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.680
short-term memory loss in Finding Dory.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.450
In fact, Andrew Stanton and screenwriter Victoria
Strouse spent nearly two years developing

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.909
the story of Dory's parents with this condition,
however, they eventually abandoned it as they

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:03.000
found it was too frustrating to have all of
Dory's family talk to each other and then

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.550
keep forgetting what they'd said.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.939
In the final film, Dory's memory about her
parents and home is triggered after she's

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:15.920
knocked out after getting caught by the undertow
while listening to a group of migrating rays

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.910
singing about going home as they pass by the
corral.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:22.359
And there were also another couple of scenes
about Dory's memories that were deleted.

00:03:22.359 --> 00:03:26.889
In the first, Dory and Marlin go to Nemo's
Parents' Day at his school to retell the tale

00:03:26.889 --> 00:03:28.010
of Finding Nemo.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:32.189
When they leave, Dory sees a seahorse family
hugging each other and she starts to feel

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:33.700
lonely and sad.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.799
In a second deleted scene, Dory spots two
blue fish, while playing around the coral,

00:03:37.799 --> 00:03:39.150
and chases after them.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:43.530
After she loses them, she ends up scared and
alone until Marlin and Nemo find her.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:49.840
The first trailer for Finding Dory also featured
another alternate scene about how Dory remembered

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:50.939
her family.

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:54.840
That showed Dory suddenly realising she'd
remembered something, then immediately getting

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.159
confused, and forgetting what she was talking
about.

00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:03.129
The Tank Gang do make an appearance in Finding
Dory's post-credits scene, but they were originally

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:06.140
set to appear in a number of scenes in the
main part of the movie.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:10.409
There was going to be a section where Marlin
and Nemo look for Dory in the shipping lanes

00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:12.719
after getting separated from her.

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:16.170
That scene would also have shown them escaping
from a group of squid by hiding in a shipping

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:21.040
container where they run into the Tank Gang,
who are trapped in a water bottle that a ship

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.259
had thrown overboard.

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:26.509
After Marlin and Nemo help the Tank Gang out
of the shipping container, Jacques and Peach

00:04:26.509 --> 00:04:29.910
sneak on to a passing tanker, take out the
guards, and then they figure out how to navigate

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.699
to the Marine Life Institute on the Californian
coast to find Dory.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:37.650
And there was even an alternate version of
this deleted scene where the Tank Gang call

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.750
on a shoal of flying fish they're friends
with who help them get to California to find

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.430
Dory.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:48.310
It's such a funny moment when we meet Hank
in the movie and he starts drinking out of

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:52.949
that coffee jug, but the animators also had
a version of Hank where it looks like he was

00:04:52.949 --> 00:04:54.440
a bit of a hot sauce addict!

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:59.460
There's some very cool concept art of Hank
hiding himself in a hot sauce bottle in a

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:05.930
similar way to when he disguised himself as
a plant in his coffee jug in the final movie.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.530
Hank also had what could have been a cool
scene cut around the moment where Hank and

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:14.350
Dory are in Destiny's pool trying to figure
out how to get to the Open Ocean exhibit.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.250
Because they don't know the way, Hank jumps
out of the pool and uses a wet-floor caution

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.570
board to disguise himself as he retrieves
a map on the ground outside.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:26.120
He then brings it back for Dory to read.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.490
The comedy duo Destiny and Bailey were originally
going to have different and much bigger parts

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:31.490
in the movie.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:35.810
According to Kaitlin Olson, who voices Destiny,
early on in the story's development, Destiny

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:39.610
and Bailey were going to be sisters who were
kind of enemies but who would come together

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.470
by the end of the movie.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:46.150
Destiny was also going to be a much more neurotic
and hypochondriac character, who screenwriter

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:50.380
Victoria Strouse has described as "constantly
on the verge of panic [and] a tremendous joy

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.380
to write."

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.710
There were also going to be a lot more jokes
about Bailey's "obsession with his head size"

00:05:55.710 --> 00:06:00.750
and Strouse has said she'd love to do a spin-off
movie with the pair.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:04.370
Although we see Dory swimming with some dolphins
at the beginning of the movie, at one point

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:08.379
in the movie's development, there was actually
going to be a group of hard-of-hearing dolphins

00:06:08.379 --> 00:06:10.159
at the Marine Life Institute.

00:06:10.159 --> 00:06:13.659
The idea was that the dolphins were being
treated for their hearing injuries there after

00:06:13.659 --> 00:06:15.420
having worked with underwater sea-mines.

